Question: Expand and simplify completely: \begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

trace.o:
To expand and simplify the given expression, we will follow the order of operations (PEMDAS/BODMAS: Parentheses/Brackets, Exponents/Orders, Multiplication and Division, and Addition and Subtraction).

Step 1: Distribute the terms inside the parentheses on the left side of the expression.
\begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2) &= x\left(x+x^2+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2) \\
&= x(x^2+3x)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

Step 2: Distribute the x on the left side of the expression.
\begin{align*}
x(x^2+3x)-3(x^2-x+2) &= x^3+3x^2-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

Step 3: Distribute the -3 on the right side of the expression.
\begin{align*}
x^3+3x^2-3(x^2-x+2) &= x^3+3x^2-3x^2+3x-6
\end{align*}

Step 4: Combine like terms.
\begin{align*}
x^3+3x^2-3x^2+3x-6 &= x^3+3x-6
\end{align*}

The final answer is: 
Final Answer: $x^3+3x-6$

